Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic faces, fur, frost, and fabric; 1890s California and Klondike.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, poplars, a wide veranda. BUCK — a massive mixed St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd, one hundred forty pounds, rich brown coat with white chest splash, proud head — bounds through grass with JUDGE MILLER'S grandsons.

(SOUND: children laughing, distant dogs barking, leaves in warm wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog from Puget Sound to San Diego.

Buck plunges into the cement swimming tank, shakes water in a glittering spray, then stalks past fox terriers like a king.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. They wanted heavy dogs, strong of muscle, with furry coats.

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

A roaring fire. Buck lies at JUDGE MILLER's feet. Dignity and ease in every line of him.

(SOUND: fire crackling, clock ticking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here he ruled. But he did not know Manuel.

EXT. ORCHARD - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads Buck on what seems a stroll. Money chinks between Manuel and a rough STRANGER at the College Park flag station.

(SOUND: coins clinking, distant train whistle)

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The rope tightens. He springs; the stranger throws him, chokes him senseless.

(SOUND: rope cinching, Buck's choked snarl, body hitting dirt)

EXT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

Buck is flung into a cagelike crate. The stranger's hand is bloody. SALOON-KEEPER peers in by tallow candle.

(SOUND: crate wood creaking, waterfront foghorns)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He could not understand. What did they want with him?

INT. EXPRESS CAR - DAY/NIGHT

Two days. Buck neither eats nor drinks. Blood-shot eyes. He hurls himself at the bars while messengers tease him.

(SOUND: locomotive shriek, wheels on rails, Buck slamming wood)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His eyes turned blood-shot. He was metamorphosed into a raging fiend.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

Four men set the crate down. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER — stout, sweater sagging at the neck — signs for it, brings hatchet and club.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting crate wood, men scrambling onto wall)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches one hundred forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again and again he charges; again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose and mouth. A final shrewd blow. He crumples.

(SOUND: club impacts, Buck's lionlike roar, body hitting packed earth)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

He brings water. Buck drinks. Raw meat, chunk by chunk, from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver. That club was his introduction to primitive law.

PERRAULT, a little weazened French-Canadian, arrives. Money passes.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, look back at receding Seattle. FRANCOIS, a swarthy half-breed, takes them below. SPITZ — big, snow-white, treacherous smile — and gloomy DAVE wait in the 'tween-decks.

(SOUND: propeller throb, gulls, cold wind rising)

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Buck's feet sink into white mush. He snorts, licks it. It bites like fire and is gone. First snow. Onlookers laugh.

(SOUND: wind over flats, men shouting, dogs yelping)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been jerked from civilization and flung into the heart of things primordial. No law but the law of club and fang.

Curly makes friendly advances to a husky. A leap, a clip of teeth — her face ripped open. Thirty huskies close in. She goes down screaming under the mass. FRANCOIS swings an axe; men with clubs scatter them. Curly lies torn in the bloody snow. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue, laughing.

(SOUND: snarls, Curly's agony, axe and clubs, then awful quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you. From that moment Buck hated Spitz with a bitter and deathless hatred.

EXT. NORTHLAND TRAIL - DAY

Harness. Buck hauls. Spitz leads. Dave and SOL-LEKS — one-eyed, battle-scarred — teach with nips. Francois's whip sings. "Ho." "Mush." Buck learns.

(SOUND: sled runners, whip crack, panted breath in cold)

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

Night: Buck learns to dig a snow hole. Buried at dawn, he bounds up in a flashing cloud, primitive fear of the trap in his eyes.

(SOUND: wind moan, Buck's ferocious snarl as he bursts free)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instincts long dead became alive. When he pointed his nose at a star and howled, it was his ancestors howling through him.

EXT. CAMP ON LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Driving snow. Spitz steals Buck's nest. Buck springs. They circle, snarling — then starving huskies raid the camp. Chaos. Spitz attacks Buck treacherously from the side in the melee.

(SOUND: wind knife-sharp, clubs on ribs, yelps, grub-box capsizing)

EXT. FROZEN RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Days of toil. Buck's feet soften; Francois rubs them, makes moccasins from his own tops. Dolly goes mad; Francois's axe ends her. Spitz tears Buck while he gasps — then takes the worst whipping of the team.

(SOUND: ice crackle, axe blow, lash on Spitz)

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - MOONLIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit. The team and sixty huskies in full cry. Buck leads, sounding the old wolf-cry. Spitz cuts across, kills the rabbit. Buck drives in upon him. To the death.

(SOUND: pack in cry, rabbit shriek, then silence but for snarls and breath)

They circle in white woods. Fang clashes fang. Buck rushes low, crunches Spitz's left foreleg — then the right. The silent circle waits. Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. Spitz disappears under the dark mass.

(SOUND: bone crunch, final struggle under the pack, then wind only)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. CAMP - DAWN

Spitz is gone. Buck, wounded, claims the lead. Francois brings Sol-leks forward. Buck drives him back. Club raised — Buck circles just out of range, wise in the way of clubs. At last Francois throws the club down. Buck swings into the lead position, laughing as dogs laugh.

(SOUND: traces snapping home, sled breaking out)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Record run. Forty miles a day. Then the mail train — heavy loads, endless toil. DAVE weakens, cries out in the traces. Taken out, he bites through Sol-leks's traces to reclaim his place. At last he cannot rise. A revolver shot behind the timber. Buck knows.

(SOUND: sled bells, Dave's mournful howl, single gunshot, then whips and continuing bells)

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Worn to one hundred fifteen pounds, the team is sold. HAL — callow, belted with cartridges — CHARLES — weak watery eyes, fierce mustache — and MERCEDES overload a top-heavy sled. Fourteen dogs. Chaos, quarrels, underfeeding.

(SOUND: whip whistling, Mercedes weeping, sled lashings creaking)

HAL
Mush on there!

The dogs cannot move the frozen sled until an onlooker forces Hal to break it out. Later: Dub shot, Outside dogs dead, Billee axed in the traces. Skeletons staggering through spring thaw.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

The wreck of a team drops. JOHN THORNTON — weathered, kind-eyed, limping slightly — whittles an axe-handle. Hal whips Buck, who will not rise. Club falls. Thornton springs.

(SOUND: whip, club on flesh, Thornton's inarticulate animal cry)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the remaining dogs pull out onto rotten ice. The sled's back end drops. A yawning hole. They are gone.

(SOUND: ice giving way, Mercedes's distant scream, then river hush)

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. RIVER BANK CAMP - DAY

Weeks. Skeet, a little Irish setter, washes Buck's wounds. Nig, huge and black, bounds in play. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shakes him, murmurs loving oaths. Buck's teeth close gently on Thornton's hand — a caress.

(SOUND: river running, birds, Thornton's low laugh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. John Thornton had saved his life — and was the ideal master.

Buck follows at heels, fears Thornton will vanish like the others. Yet deep in the forest a call sounds. He plunges toward it, then love draws him back to the fire.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON / STREET - DAY

Sixty below. MATTHEWSON's sled holds a thousand pounds of flour, runners frozen. Wager laid. Thornton kneels, cheek to Buck's.

(SOUND: crowd murmur, intense cold silence)

JOHN THORNTON
(whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee! Haw! Mush!

Buck breaks the sled out, walks it a hundred yards. Roar of the crowd. Thornton falls to his knees, shaking him, cursing him softly and lovingly.

(SOUND: runners cracking free, then men shouting, hats in air)

EXT. GOLD CLAIM CAMP - DAY

With Hans and Pete they seek a lost mine, find gold instead — moose-hide sacks piled like firewood beside the claim. Buck muses by the fire. The short-legged hairy man of dreams. The call grows.

(SOUND: pan washing gravel, night insects, distant wolf howl)

EXT. FOREST DIVIDE - DUSK

A long lean timber wolf. Buck runs with the wild brother across the divide, then remembers Thornton and turns back. The wolf's mournful howl fades.

(SOUND: paws on moss and stone, wind in spruce, fading wolf howl)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thornton alone held him. The rest of mankind was as nothing.

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH CAMP - DAY

Buck returns from killing a great bull moose. Fresh trail. Nig dead with an arrow. A sled-dog thrashing. Hans feathered with arrows. YEEHAT warriors dance about the wreckage.

(SOUND: sing-song chant, then Buck's fearful roaring)

Buck becomes a hurricane. Throat after throat. Panic. The Yeehats flee, crying of an Evil Spirit. Pete dead in his blankets. Skeet at the pool's edge. No trace leads away from the water. John Thornton is gone.

(SOUND: arrows, screams, then wind over desolation)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death left a great void in him. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. MOONLIT CLEARING - NIGHT

The wolf pack pours in like a silvery flood. Buck stands motionless. The boldest leaps — neck broken. Others slash and fail. At last the long lean wild brother advances. They touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf howls. Buck howls. He runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

(SOUND: pack yelps rising to full chorus, then the long song of the pack)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The years were not many when the Yeehats noted wolves with brown on muzzle and a rift of white down the chest. They tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack.

EXT. OPEN SPACE AMONG TREES - SUMMER DAY

A great, gloriously coated wolf stands where yellow dust once spilled from rotted moose-hide sacks. He muses, howls once long and mournfully, and departs.

(SOUND: single mournful howl, breeze in grass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But when the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
